                  <provenance type="#c"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_34">Bath, Somerset.
                        Benedictine cathedral priory of St Saviour, St Peter, and St Paul.
                        </orgName><p>The ten Royal manuscripts belonged to Cranmer. 3 B. xvi is
                        probably in the same hand as 6 C. xi. The marginalia in 5 D. iv, 5 D. v, 5
                        D. vi, and perhaps 5 D. vii are in the same hand as marginalia in 6 C. xi. 5
                        B. ii resembles 6 B. i in script and decoration. 6 C. iii has been added to
                        this number since MLGB2: 'Its scribal evidence makes a Bath origin, and
                        probably provenance too, virtually certain' (M. Gullick in The Medieval
                        Book. Glosses From Friends &amp; Colleagues of Christopher De Hamel (2010),
                        3--10, at 8 n. 11).</p><p>At end (fol. 123r): 'Iohannes Dunster ob'. Dunster
                        prauus. Iohannes Salisbury i d. Salisbury nunquam vigil.' John Salisbury was
                        sub-almoner in 1447 (Reg. Bekyngton, ii. 435); John Dunster was precentor in
                        1458 (Reg. Bekyngton, i. 309). Are these notes of fines in
                     chapter?</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Thomas Cranmer (1489--1556), archbishop of
                     Canterbury.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">John Lumley, first Baron Lumley (c.
                     1533--1609).</provenance>
               </history>
